UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 34, Lesson 1: Sample spaces', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Year 7 students develop sample spaces with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks. Represent the situation, choose an appropriate procedure, calculate carefully and verify with an inverse, estimate or diagram. Catalogue alignment uses MA4-PRO-C-01; detailed content-point mapping remains pending.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nFind two probabilities or sample-space listings for sample spaces and simplify fractions.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: equally likely outcomes handled correctly; fractions simplified.\n\nWATCH FOR\nListing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nIndependently complete a substantial practice set on sample spaces: at least eight varied items (including one multi-step or contextual item), show methods, check three answers, and write a five-sentence reflection naming one strategy and one error to watch.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Year 7 students develop sample spaces with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks. Represent the situation, choose an appropriate procedure, calculate carefully and verify with an inverse, estimate or diagram. Catalogue alignment uses MA4-PRO-C-01; detailed content-point mapping remains pending.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA4-PRO-C-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Independently complete a substantial practice set on sample spaces: at least eight varied items (including one multi-step or contextual item), show methods, check three answers, and write a five-sentence reflection namin Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for sample spaces; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1.. Correction uses Strong answer: equally likely outcomes handled correctly;. Why: restores accurate sample spaces.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses sample spaces with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with sample spaces, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for sample spaces, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about sample spaces, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for sample spaces. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","equally likely outcomes handled correctly"],"question":"Find two probabilities or sample-space listings for sample spaces and simplify fractions. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for sample spaces; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["equally likely outcomes handled correctly","Careful use of strategy and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1","Year 7 students develop sample spaces with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with sample spaces?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named strategy and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1","Name strategy and explain why it matters only, without linking it to sample spaces","Apply sample spaces to new material and name the strategy and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies sample spaces with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Write only a final number with no working","Change digits to look neater without checking place value","Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on sample spaces?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught sample spaces method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore units and treat 3 and 6 as interchangeable","equally likely outcomes handled correctly","Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1","Read 3 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value"],"question":"Using the method for sample spaces, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically","Delete units so the answers appear to match","Average the two answers without checking either"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a sample spaces problem with numbers such as 3 and 6, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same sample spaces method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter","Retype the worked model numbers 3 and 6 as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1"],"question":"Solve a multi-step sample spaces problem with different values from the model, justify each step, and check reasonableness.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["equally likely outcomes handled correctly","Label units when comparing 3 with 6","Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1","Year 7 students develop sample spaces with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for sample spaces?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for sample spaces, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses sample spaces with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for sample spaces; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about sample spaces, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with sample spaces, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1.. Correction uses Strong answer: equally likely outcomes handled correctly;. Why: restores accurate sample spaces.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-mat-y7-w34-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise sample spaces.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Probability","success_criteria":["I can use sample spaces with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify strategy and explain why it matters.","I can practise check independently on a new prompt.","I can notice reasonableness needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Year 7 students develop sample spaces with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Year 7 students develop sample spaces with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about sample spaces with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample for Sample spaces: P(even on fair die)=3/6=1/2; two-coin sample space HH,HT,TH,TT; experimental relative frequency approaches theoretical with more trials.\n\nTRY FIRST\nFind two probabilities or sample-space listings for sample spaces and simplify fractions.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: equally likely outcomes handled correctly; fractions simplified.","explain":"Year 7 students develop sample spaces with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks. Represent the situation, choose an appropriate procedure, calculate carefully and verify with an inverse, estimate or diagram. Catalogue alignment uses MA4-PRO-C-01; detailed content-point mapping remains pending.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Listing outcomes unequally or writing probabilities outside 0–1. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Sample spaces.","caption":"Then check.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Represent</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">diagram/expression</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Calculate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">careful steps</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example for Sample spaces: P(even on fair die)=3/6=1/2; two-coin sample space HH,HT,TH,TT; experimental relative frequen Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example for Sample spaces: P(even on fair die)=3/6=1/2; two-coin sample space HH,HT,TH,TT; experimental relative frequen Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about sample spaces with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample for Sample spaces: P(even on fair die)=3/6=1/2; two-coin sample space HH,HT,TH,TT; experimental relative frequency approaches theoretical with more trials. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Reasonableness.","caption":"Units matter.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/200